AccessDenied with valid credentials|A workload authenticates but an AWS API operation is denied.|aws sts get-caller-identity|Confirm the actual role, account, action and resource. Inspect identity/resource policies, relevant conditions and explicit-deny controls rather than only the attached allow policy.|Correct the narrowly missing permission or trust relationship in code. Do not grant administrator access to conceal an unexplained deny.|Repeat the intended operation with the workload identity and verify access to unrelated resources remains denied.
Private instance cannot reach internet|A private workload cannot download required updates.|aws ec2 describe-route-tables --filters Name=association.subnet-id,Values=SUBNET_ID|Check the effective subnet route including the main table, NAT path, public-side routing, security controls and DNS. Distinguish DNS failure from TCP egress failure.|Repair the specific route or egress dependency. Prefer a service endpoint for supported private service access when that matches the design.|Test name resolution and the required outbound request from the workload while preserving inbound isolation.
ALB returns 503|An Application Load Balancer has no usable backend for a route.|aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn TARGET_GROUP_ARN|Inspect target registration, health reasons, listener routing and target ports. Compare the check path with actual application readiness.|Restore healthy targets or correct routing/check configuration without masking a broken application. Keep enough capacity during replacement.|Verify target health and representative requests through the same listener and hostname clients use.
Scaling does not add capacity|An Auto Scaling group wants more instances but remains below desired capacity.|aws autoscaling describe-auto-scaling-groups --auto-scaling-group-names GROUP_NAME|Inspect scaling activity, launch-template errors, quotas, subnet address availability and instance health failures.|Fix the evidenced launch or capacity constraint; use approved alternate capacity where needed. Avoid increasing desired count repeatedly without resolving the blocker.|Confirm new instances become healthy and workload latency improves, not merely that instance count rises.
S3 object unexpectedly unavailable|An object read fails after a deployment or cleanup.|aws s3api get-bucket-versioning --bucket BUCKET|Check key spelling, caller permissions, delete markers, lifecycle rules and encryption-key access. Access errors can obscure whether an object exists.|Restore the intended retained version if appropriate or repair the specific access rule. Do not make the bucket public as a diagnostic shortcut.|Read the intended object as the application role and confirm its version/content and restricted access.
Duplicate queue processing|Customers receive duplicate effects from an SQS consumer.|aws sqs get-queue-attributes --queue-url QUEUE_URL --attribute-names All|Compare processing duration with visibility timeout and inspect retry/delete behavior. Delivery semantics require duplicate-safe processing even without an obvious timeout.|Make the business operation idempotent, tune or extend visibility appropriately and delete only after successful processing.|Replay a message and simulate a worker interruption in a safe test; confirm one intended business effect and bounded retries.
Dead-letter queue grows|Messages repeatedly fail and accumulate in a DLQ.|aws sqs get-queue-attributes --queue-url QUEUE_URL --attribute-names All|Inspect representative failed payloads securely, consumer errors and recent schema changes. Determine whether failures are poison messages or a shared dependency outage.|Fix the consumer or dependency, then redrive a small verified batch with rate limits and duplicate protection.|Confirm replayed messages complete, DLQ growth stops and normal queue latency remains acceptable.
KMS decryption denied|The application can locate encrypted data but cannot decrypt it.|aws kms describe-key --key-id KEY_ID|Check key state, region, key policy, IAM permissions and encryption-context conditions. Data-read permission and decrypt permission are separate.|Restore the intended key availability or narrowly correct policy/context usage. Do not create a new unrelated key expecting it to decrypt old ciphertext.|Read and decrypt as the actual workload identity and confirm unauthorized identities remain blocked.
Database failover leaves application errors|RDS has completed failover but the application still reports connection failures.|aws rds describe-db-instances --db-instance-identifier DB_ID|Inspect endpoint resolution, stale pooled connections, retry deadlines and database health. Existing connections may not move to the new writer.|Refresh invalid connections and use bounded reconnect behavior compatible with transaction idempotency. Avoid replaying uncertain writes blindly.|Execute representative reads and writes through the normal endpoint and verify consistency and error-rate recovery.
DR restore misses objective|A regional recovery restores resources but not a usable service within the target time.|aws sts get-caller-identity|Verify recovery-account access, data freshness, keys, quotas, DNS and dependency order. Measure the complete path rather than only snapshot restore time.|Fix the slow or missing recovery steps in infrastructure and runbooks, and preprovision justified prerequisites for the required RTO/RPO.|Repeat an isolated recovery exercise and record successful user transactions, recovered data age and end-to-end elapsed time.
